                        "sense_id": "2bef4151-6f50-304a-9180-8b79817bd891",
                        "entry_id": "e5ec28c6-f1db-36bf-a2f9-5d952097aa6e",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Grand lémurien de Madagascar, de la famille des indridés, diurne et folivore."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "2d4764cf-0f1a-49d8-abb0-24e46696e830",
                                "sense_id": "2bef4151-6f50-304a-9180-8b79817bd891",
                                "text": "L’hôte le plus recherché de la réserve est l’indri, le plus grand des lémuriens, appelé babakoto (babakout) en malgache."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "5457536c-a578-4f68-a585-8da24cafc021",
                                "sense_id": "2bef4151-6f50-304a-9180-8b79817bd891",
                                "text": "Concernant les organisations sociales, on observe des groupes strictement familiaux rassemblant les parents monogames, la progéniture et éventuellement les jeunes de la génération précédente (comme chez les titis, indris, gabbons et siamangs)."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "15996de5-02eb-49df-8580-562169d1eb12",
                        "source_entry_id": "e5ec28c6-f1db-36bf-a2f9-5d952097aa6e",
                        "source_sense_id": "2bef4151-6f50-304a-9180-8b79817bd891",
                        "target_lang_word": "Indri",
                        "target_lang_code": "de",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "Indri",
                                "target_lang_code": "de",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "3b27d21c-6870-3d3c-a39f-057866ed1b6b",
                                        "entry_id": "33b4dc06-2ed2-3c9b-b120-75434caf1b76",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Primatenart aus der Familie der Lemuren, die im Nordosten Madagaskars vorkommt"
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ca7ecd21-b5f4-4fc0-8672-bd5310ec5dc8",
                                                "sense_id": "3b27d21c-6870-3d3c-a39f-057866ed1b6b",
                                                "text": "„Mit einer Körperhöhe von 80 cm ist der Indri (Indri Indri) heute die größte noch existierende Lemurenart.“"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "95855715-0189-44e8-8e0e-bce72e9db9ef",
                                                "sense_id": "3b27d21c-6870-3d3c-a39f-057866ed1b6b",
                                                "text": "„Da sein Lebensraum zunehmend zerstört wird, ist der Indri vom Aussterben bedroht.“"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "caa32b00-2b74-4609-a987-4f9f05df6738",
                                                "sense_id": "3b27d21c-6870-3d3c-a39f-057866ed1b6b",
                                                "text": "„Zur Familie der Indridae gehören der kleine Wollmaki und der Indri, der von den Eingeborenen wegen seiner lauten Stimme Amboanala oder Hund des Waldes genannt wird.“"
                                            }
                                        ],
                                        "sense_index_json": "\"1\""
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "372f8b48-1831-43fa-b05a-facd7827219d",
                        "source_entry_id": "e5ec28c6-f1db-36bf-a2f9-5d952097aa6e",
                        "source_sense_id": "2bef4151-6f50-304a-9180-8b79817bd891",
                        "target_lang_word": "indri",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "indri",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "1e1bf969-272e-3bbd-8483-849017540106",
                                        "entry_id": "76855af3-6aa0-3d86-b5c1-e58d5265f397",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "One of the largest living lemurs (Indri indri), native to Madagascar."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "5481d63f-7eb9-49a7-9d4d-c72e5a0f5bea",
                                                "sense_id": "1e1bf969-272e-3bbd-8483-849017540106",
                                                "text": "Indri, the ſecond new genus, conſiſts at preſent but of two ſpecies, the Linnæan lemur indri and the laniger; the former of which is lemur brevicaudatus of Geoffroy [i.e., Étienne Geoffroy Saint-Hilaire]; and the latter indri longicaudatus of the ſame author."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "a1df9517-6563-4ac2-8bb8-4ad2b0195366",
                                                "sense_id": "1e1bf969-272e-3bbd-8483-849017540106",
                                                "text": "The indris are distuinguished by having only thirty teeth. [...] The Indri (Indris laniger) is the largest known species of the lemurine Quadrumana, measuring when erect more than three feet in height, and is of very remarkable form. [...] The indris inhabit the forests of Madagascar. They are very gentle, and although not remarkable for intelligence, are trained by the natives (who call the indri, \"the man of the woods\"), to hunt, probably for birds."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "032961d9-f033-429b-8860-ded354956978",
                                                "sense_id": "1e1bf969-272e-3bbd-8483-849017540106",
                                                "text": "The Indri (Indri indri; top left), the largest prosimian at up to three feet long (excluding tail) and 22 pounds, eats plant matter."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "95c5802b-2f9c-4a6a-9b70-53dd3002664e",
                                                "sense_id": "1e1bf969-272e-3bbd-8483-849017540106",
                                                "text": "The indri inhabits coastal and montane rainforest from sea level to about 1,800 meters."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "9c7d7177-bf54-4b53-9b72-ca5b1d5c6200",
                                                "sense_id": "1e1bf969-272e-3bbd-8483-849017540106",
                                                "text": "Indris are known for their calls, some of which are truly eerie and can be heard for miles in the dense undergrowth."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
